  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Five hundred men worked fifteen years to build one hill. Silbury, in Wiltshire, stands thirty-nine metres high and holds 248,000 cubic metres of chalk.",
        "visual_query": "wiltshire chalk downland grass mound",
        "visual_fallback": "green mound"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That is eighteen million estimated man-hours, around 2400 BC. And almost no prehistoric artefacts have ever come out of it.",
        "visual_query": "english countryside prehistoric earthwork field",
        "visual_fallback": "rolling farmland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So people dug in anyway. A duke sank a shaft from the summit in 1776, and tunnels followed in 1849 and 1968.",
        "visual_query": "dark excavation tunnel chalk walls",
        "visual_fallback": "dark tunnel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The core gave up clay, flints, turf, antler tines and ox bones. Then in 2002 that 1776 shaft collapsed in heavy rain.",
        "visual_query": "antler bone flint stone artefacts",
        "visual_fallback": "old bones"
      },
      {
        "narration": "English Heritage backfilled the tunnels in 2007. Jim Leary and David Field concluded the point was never the finished mound, but the building of it.",
        "visual_query": "avebury wiltshire green fields aerial",
        "visual_fallback": "english fields"
      }
    ],
